WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.600
We have a lot of choices
to make about our diet.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.480
Add to that, doing
the right thing

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.620
when it comes
to preventing

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.940
and treating a
chronic disease,

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.280
fighting a virus,
or losing weight,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.740
and suddenly our
nutrition choices

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.140
can seem almost
overwhelming.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.480
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.700
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.860
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.980
Today we take a look
at how some employers

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.740
are taking steps to help
us become healthier.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.700
In our first story, we look at how
workplace wellness programs

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.280
report an average
ROI of 3,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.460
returning $3 for
every $1 invested.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.080
An impressive number of studies
have shown that lifestyle is

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.220
the root cause of what ails us,
particularly for chronic conditions.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.700
The studies also show that
changing one’s lifestyle can have

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.800
a dramatic effect on
health improvement,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.980
both in the prevention
and treatment of disease.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.880
So, given the benefits of
lifestyle medicine interventions,

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.440
it would seem that our
health care system

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.600
would rush to embrace
this movement;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.560
however, little could be
further from the truth.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.400
Now, I know we have a
for-profit healthcare system,

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.460
but even just from a
dollars-and-cents perspective,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.520
it may make sense—and dollars!

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.760
A majority of Americans have
health care insurance through their

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.780
employers, the cost for which
is rising at an alarming rate.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.080
Health insurance premiums
continue to go up year after year.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.800
Unfortunately, all the money
we’re spending has not translated

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.920
into healthier employees
or healthier Americans.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.640
We spend more on
healthcare per person by far,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.760
yet the U.S. population
is not healthy.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.040
By 2014, our life expectancy had
slipped down to 43rd in the world.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.660
And since then, our life
expectancy declined year after year.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.420
It’s not just that other
countries are doing better,

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.660
we now may be living
shorter and shorter lives.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.020
And unhealthier lives—again
even just from a financial

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.020
bottom-line perspective,
hundreds of billions

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.440
in productivity lost, beyond
all the healthcare costs.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.320
One employee gets
diabetes, and it costs

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.180
about $10,000 more per year.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.920
So, if a self-insured company

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.260
could prevent or reverse
that case, they could save

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.020
the company a lot of money,
raising the possibility

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.440
that workplace wellness programs
could not just be cost-neutral,

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.080
in other words pay for
themselves, but actually save

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.720
the company money, even
make the company money.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.800
This brings up the concept of
ROI, or return on investment.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.100
That’s how much money you get
back for every dollar spent.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:40.560
So, an ROI of 3 to 1 means every
dollar you spend yields $3 back;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.580
so, by investing in such a program
you’d end up tripling your money.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.320
You don’t know though...
until you put it to the test.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.560
Johnson &amp; Johnson, for example,
claimed that investing

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.380
in employee wellness
yielded around 2 to 4 dollars

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.640
for each dollar they spent.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.640
Citibank estimated their
return on investment

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.460
was $5 saved for every dollar spent.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.540
A compilation of a handful of
such studies found an average ROI

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.180
of more than three dollars
of healthcare cost savings

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.220
for each dollar spent
on health promotion.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.000
A more recent review including
more than 20 studies came up

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.940
with a return on investment
exceeding 5 to 1,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.300
though an even more recent
review out of Harvard looking at

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.720
more than 30 studies found
the ROI closer to three dollars.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.780
Spend one dollar, get
three back. Not a bad deal.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.220
One heavy manufacturing company
reported an outstanding

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.340
34-to-1 return on investment,

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.220
and that’s just direct
healthcare costs.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.160
Some of their indirect costs,
like lost workdays and disability,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.420
fell even more.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.400
Then, you can imagine other
potential benefits—happier employees,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.640
more productive employees, making
them more likely to stick around.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.580
So, do companies that
invest in health promotion

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.500
have a competitive advantage?

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.660
Publicly traded
companies that got

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.660
Corporate Health Achievement
Awards outperformed

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.460
the S&amp;P 500 by 40 percent.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.040
C. Everett Koop Award winners

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.620
outperformed the S&amp;P 500 two to one.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.080
Those scoring higher on workplace

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.840
health promotion best
practices, same trend.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.360
And the same thing most
recently with companies

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.000
investing in an internal
culture of health.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.780
Of course, instead of
healthier employees leading to

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:25.500
a more profitable company, maybe
more profitable companies

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.980
have more money to spend to
make employees healthier.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.220
Is there a link between
stock market price growth

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.260
and having a great
employee wellness program?

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.340
Maybe, but correlation
doesn’t mean causation.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.040
Maybe prioritizing
wellness is just a sign

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.280
of a great management team,
and that’s the real reason

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:45.600
they’re raking in the dough.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.980
Even most of the ROI studies
lacked an adequate control group.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.520
You can’t just compare the
costs of employees that signed up

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.060
versus those that didn’t,
because maybe healthier people

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.280
are just drawn to such
programs in the first place.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.540
That’s why you need randomized
clinical trials to really

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.480
put it to the test,
which we’ll cover, next.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.880
How much is a human life worth?

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.080
Ideally, we’d like to think
there is no price tag.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.080
Each precious life is
of inestimable value,

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.940
but there are limited resources
and policy decisions to be made,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.540
which leads to fascinating
papers like this:

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.980
the cost effectiveness of 500
life-saving interventions,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:37.920
587 to be exact, with some costing
more than 10 billion dollars

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.440
per year of life saved.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.380
Overall, the average
intervention costs $42,000,

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.700
which is how much it costs to
save a year of someone’s life

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.460
putting flashing lights
at railroad crossings.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.340
So, how could you justify
not installing them?

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.100
Well, you could save more
lives with the same money

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.680
if you added grooved
pavement on highways.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.620
But if you really cared about
saving drivers, you could save

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.280
nearly a hundred times more
lives investing the same money

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.940
in media campaigns to encourage
people to wear their seatbelts.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.660
But if you really want value,
if you want to prevent

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.320
bangs for the least bucks,
there are some interventions

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.200
that save more resources
than they cost.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:23.640
Yeah, most interventions cost
$10,000 or more per year

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.574
of life saved, but check out these.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.540
Studies, for example, suggest

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:30.360
“Workplace Wellness Programs
Can Generate Savings.”

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.000
Not just saving more than
you’re spending but potentially

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.220
tripling your money
by investing in wellness.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.360
But since employee participation
is almost always voluntary,

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.660
this introduces the
specter of selection bias,

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:44.100
where healthier people
are just drawn

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.660
to wellness programs
in the first place.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.460
And indeed, during the year
prior to a wellness intervention,

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.240
those who would later
sign up already had lower

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.400
medical expenditures and
healthier behaviors

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.360
than future nonparticipants; so,
no wonder they could end up

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.880
doing better than their
nonparticipating colleagues

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.280
even if the wellness
program had zero benefit.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.760
Thankfully, there are an
increased number of randomized

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.640
controlled trials of wellness
programs to see if we can prove

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.620
cause-and-effect, to
answer the question

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.380
“Do Workplace Health
Promotion Programs Work?,”

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:19.320
particularly important given that
the workplace wellness industry

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.320
is now a multibillion-dollar industry

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.380
One of the most recent
randomized controlled trials

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.200
was published in the Journal of the
American Medical Association.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.380
Thousands of employees were
effectively randomized to a

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.140
workplace wellness program
at BJ’s Wholesale Club,

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:36.480
and although program
participants reported

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.640
they were engaging in more
positive health behaviors,

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.380
such as getting regular exercise,

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:44.760
there were no significant
differences in clinical measures

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.280
of health or health care
spending and utilization.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:51.300
The authors suggested we may
have to temper our expectations

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.480
about the financial return on
investment that wellness programs

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.580
can deliver, at least
in the short term.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:00.960
Now, some argue that even a
break-even program, that is,

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.720
one that does not impose
additional costs to the company

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.660
but produces signiﬁcant
population health improvement,

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:10.320
might be a worthy investment, but
perhaps the growing evidence

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.380
that demonstrates limited or
no concrete wellness program

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:16.980
effects should encourage wellness
companies and employers

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.640
to critically assess the programs
and try to improve them.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.780
In a survey of 162 company
wellness programs, for example,

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:27.780
only a third offered
nutrition counseling.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.240
And even with counseling,
it’s more than simply trying

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.340
to convince people to take
better care of themselves.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.940
It requires that the organization
creates an environment

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:39.120
where leading a healthy lifestyle
is the “default” option.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.960
What are they promoting in the
company cafeteria, for example?

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.200
What’s being served up at meetings
and in vending machines?

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.580
Yeah, a lifestyle management
program at PepsiCo failed to save

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.940
the company money, but somehow
I doubt one of the components

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.600
of PepsiCo’s wellness program,
Healthy Living, was telling

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.980
employees not to eat or drink
any of their own products.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.380
In fact, if you go back
to that BJ’s case,

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:07.140
yes, a few percentages
more employees said

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.480
they were more regularly exercising,

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:12.960
but when it came to nutrition,
it was a complete flop,

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.360
in terms of things like
drinking less soda

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:16.680
or more fruits and vegetables.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:20.040
No wonder their health hardly budged.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.460
In a systematic review of
systematic reviews on the

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:25.860
effectiveness of dietary
workplace interventions,

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:28.500
the average improvement in
fruit and vegetable intake

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:32.340
combined was 0.7 portions a day.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:35.280
No wonder these programs
aren’t doing better.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.300
In Wealth of Nations, Adam Smith
wrote that workers

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.780
are less likely to be productive
“when they are frequently sick

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.580
than when they are
generally in good health.”

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:49.260
Sickness “cannot fail to diminish
the produce of their industry.”

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:56.833
Maybe they just need more produce
than 0.7 servings a day.

00:09:57.642 --> 00:10:00.120
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.880
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:05.460
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:09.780
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

